 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, not yet.** Inflation in Australia is above the RBA's 2–3% target and has been rising for about a year.\n\n**Headline CPI (annual change)**\n- **4.0% in August 2026**, the latest month available. It's been above 3% every month since July 2025 and peaked at **4.6% in March 2026**.\n- It had been inside the target band in mid-2025, bottoming at **1.9% in June 2025**.\n\n**Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred measure of underlying inflation)**\n- **3.6% in July and August 2026**. It has risen steadily from **2.8% in June 2025**.\n- This matters more than the headline number. Headline inflation bounces around from month to month, but underlying inflation has risen almost without a break, so the price pressure is broad-based and not just a few volatile items.\n\n**How the RBA has responded**\n- It cut the cash rate from 4.35% to **3.60%** during 2025, when inflation looked under control.\n- It reversed those cuts in 2026. The monthly average cash rate went from 3.60% in January to **4.35% by June**, which looks like about three hikes, and it stayed around there through **September (4.36%)**. The tiny uptick in September is a monthly average, so it may hide a change late in the month, but I can't confirm that from this data.\n\n**Bottom line:** As of August 2026, both headline (4.0%) and underlying (3.6%) inflation are above the top of the target band, and underlying inflation hadn't clearly peaked. The RBA undid all of its 2025 cuts, which shows it doesn't think inflation is under control either. The best sign would be the trimmed mean turning down from 3.6% in the next few releases.",
